      "latency_ms": 15339.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 558,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on the surface of Earth. It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Chemical Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **States of Matter:** Water is unique because it naturally exists on Earth in three distinct states:\n  * **Liquid:** The standard form found in oceans, rivers, and rain.\n  * **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F).\n  * **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F).\n\n### 2. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater has several unique properties that make it vital for life:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances (like salts, sugars, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our bodies transport nutrients and how oceans stay salty.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of tall trees).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and the internal temperature of living organisms.\n* **Expanding When Freezing:** Unlike most substances, water expands when it turns into ice, making ice less dense than liquid water. This is why ice floats, allowing aquatic life to survive under frozen lakes during the winter.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Biological View\n* **The Engine of Life:** The human body is made of about 60% water, and living cells require water to carry out their chemical reactions. Without water, metabolic processes stop.\n* **Earth’s Life Support:** About 71% of the Earth's surface is covered in water, mostly in oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). It drives weather patterns, ocean currents, and the water cycle (evaporation, condensation, precipitation).\n\n**In short:** Water is a simple molecule that acts as the lifeblood of our planet—shaping our geography, regulating our climate, and making biological life possible."
